THOUGHT: The field `buffer` is currently non-annotated, so NullAway treats it as @NonNull and complains when it’s set to null in `release()`. The simplest fix that doesn’t change behavior is to mark `buffer` as @Nullable and ensure all uses are guarded by null checks. However, the code already checks `if (buffer != null)` in `release()` but not elsewhere, so making it nullable might introduce new NullAway errors at other uses. A better approach is to avoid assigning null at all: we can keep `buffer` @NonNull and instead track whether it has been released with a boolean flag, only returning it to the pool once. I’ll modify the class to add a `boolean released` field, use it in `release()`, and remove `buffer = null;`, then run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/glide && sed -i '15a\  private boolean released;' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/load/data/BufferedOutputStream.java && sed -i '95,100c\  private void release() {
    if (!released) {
      released = true;
      arrayPool.put(buffer);
    }
  }
}' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/load/data/BufferedOutputStream.java && ./gradlew library:compileDebugJavaWithJavac -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "BufferedOutputStream" -n new_build_output.log || true```

